    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "With increasing attention from global scholarship on Qing history, more research on diverse topics related to Qing history has been accomplished. Despite the excellence of existing scholarship, detailed studies and examinations of Non-Han figures, as well as their political choices and life trajectories in the late Qing to the Republican periods, are still lacking. This project investigates individual experiences and decision-making during an unprecedented period of extreme uncertainty. This paper focuses primarily on a case study of Liangbi, a member of the Aisin Gioro family who participated in the Qing educational mission in Japan. After returning to China, Liangbi continued his career at the center of the Qing court in Beijing and supported late Qing military reforms. Through an analysis of Liangbi’s early years and his death by assassination in 1912, this project explores the dynamic process of identity-building and decision-making across complex political and social situations in multiethnic and transnational contexts from the late 19th to the early 20th century. It aims to open discussions on Manchu-Han relations and frontier issues in the late Qing and encourage further research on Non-Han figures in the late Qing and Republican eras. This project is not intended to be a brief biography of Liangbi. Rather, it explores how individuals navigated and made their way through a rapidly changing and uncertain world. The meaning of history can vary greatly for different people: it can be about debate, critical thinking, time, evidence, or passion. Many individual cases similar to Liangbi’s deserve further study to portray the restless transition from the late Qing to the early Republic and to capture the nuanced moments of human emotion in experiencing both excitement and powerlessness. While their history might be in the past for us, it was their reality at the time. Thus, it is crucial not to impose present-day biases on the past. Whether famous or not, known or unknown, remembered or forgotten, individuals like Liangbi were making history just as we are making our own today.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
